  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Prolonged stretch of dry weather across southern New \nEngland, potentially lasting through Halloween. Afternoon \ntemperatures moderate through tomorrow, followed by a brief \ncooldown Tuesday and Wednesday before milder weather returns \nlate in the week.\n\nRadiational cooling expected once again tonight, though not as cold \nas last night given slightly warmer temperatures aloft. 925 mb \ntemperatures reach around 12C overnight, then continue to improve \nthrough the day Monday a few more degrees (14C). Highs Monday are \nexpected to reach the upper 60s and some clouds may move in ahead of \nan approaching cold front. This cold front is expected to be quite \ndry otherwise, given the persistent high pressure and dry air \npresent. With its passage, winds shift more N to NW heading into \nMonday night and a mix of some low to high clouds move in, which \nwill end up limiting radiational cooling. Lows Monday night are \nexpected to generally be in the 30s and 40s.\n\nHigh pressure will continue to govern Southern New England's weather \nthrough the upcoming workweek, leading to a continued stretch of dry \nand seasonable autumn weather. We get a brief cooldown on Tuesday in \nthe wake of the cold front from Monday night; with 925 mb temps \naround +2 to +5C, highs mainly in the mid 50s. We get into a \nwarming/modifying trend to temperatures through the rest of the \nworkweek. By Thursday and Friday, highs could reach well into the \n60s to lower 70s.  \n\nAnother mostly dry cold front then passes to our north Friday night. \nThis feature again looks to only bring some enhanced cloudiness for \nFriday night, but the chances for rainfall associated with this \nfeature looks minimal. It will bring another shot of cool air for \nthe weekend with blustery conditions, with temperatures falling back \ninto the 50s to lower 60s for the weekend.",
